      {
        "id": "p19-q1-k",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "k",
        "prompt": "$y=-x^2+2x+4$ and $2y+x=1$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(-1,1)$ and $(\\tfrac72,-\\tfrac54)$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}-x^2+2x+4&=\\frac{1-x}{2}\\\\-2x^2+4x+8&=1-x\\\\2x^2-5x-7&=0\\\\(2x-7)(x+1)&=0\\\\x&=\\frac72,-1\\end{align*}$$\nSubstituting into $y=\\tfrac{1-x}{2}$ gives $(\\tfrac72,-\\tfrac54)$ and $(-1,1)$. The parabola has vertex $(1,5)$, $y$-intercept $(0,4)$ and $x$-intercepts $(1-\\sqrt5,0)$ and $(1+\\sqrt5,0)$; the line has intercepts $(1,0)$ and $(0,\\tfrac12)$.",
